#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9eb204e-c70d-54d4-b742-f513048a08b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353349} {:answer/id #uuid "0cee747c-bc60-5e87-a679-0c290c9c6034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353350} {:answer/id #uuid "a98ac62f-f058-57ac-8335-a15f9724ddb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353351} {:answer/id #uuid "012dab08-5bc7-5382-be68-12dcc9a3542b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353352} {:answer/id #uuid "0974819d-0b6d-5aad-9dba-2ce912959e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a98ac62f-f058-57ac-8335-a15f9724ddb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353351} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7501d8-9c50-5faa-86db-a943f1bb2057" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0432fb7b-7daa-53b5-90f8-a5e72262915a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334867" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have ${e}^{x}≈1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}≈\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nThus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}≈1$ near $x=0,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d868102-8db9-5be3-bdc0-5855ebfcbf2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353356} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd1d1ce-eda9-5542-9461-b2f4d925c669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353357} {:answer/id #uuid "e37ade1e-058a-53f8-ba30-aa266c62084a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353358} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac76b12-90e2-5a34-b485-ca858e01aa31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353359} {:answer/id #uuid "418c4797-b1f1-5f86-98be-0f6440a5593f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e37ade1e-058a-53f8-ba30-aa266c62084a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353358} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2f3c6f6-849b-58b1-997c-8d2d84e818ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb2dcc43-be7e-5d30-a224-0c93857a866f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335020" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{2}+5+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b841cdab-5473-5eeb-a02a-1d1acbae2984" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Limit Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bb3ddaf-3f36-59dc-8dcc-9e9ef1352442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353363} {:answer/id #uuid "696b2e76-27c8-5820-a7c4-7e01deae5cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353364} {:answer/id #uuid "d9464860-7fd9-5e3c-9c31-dfdc3e138629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353365} {:answer/id #uuid "9a83a6aa-951d-5e9b-a030-349dd4ebfbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353366} {:answer/id #uuid "d1eebab7-2f00-55e8-bcd5-3019876eab52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1eebab7-2f00-55e8-bcd5-3019876eab52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353367} :answer-field/id #uuid "eac693ab-03c7-5c64-ac12-0b75caae58b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "265a0e7d-50b1-599b-b8a6-59c58750a06d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335048" :question/problem "Suppose $f(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\infty +0 \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of Big-O"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86aa0f70-e821-5a83-90ba-7f58b7c94359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155347} {:answer/id #uuid "d00037b7-7362-5f84-a3cd-4b425c145ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155348} {:answer/id #uuid "25b40a8c-70a3-569a-a96c-47781c7d41a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155349} {:answer/id #uuid "3dcddb7b-1fb3-566b-ac51-44d1cc090af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155350} {:answer/id #uuid "70ada491-e1a2-534c-8276-40c3b2395d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86aa0f70-e821-5a83-90ba-7f58b7c94359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155347} :answer-field/id #uuid "671be263-7309-5ffe-861e-fdece5e1fe8c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155346} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcfa926c-b9c6-5676-b87d-f21f013c200b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155362} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c7c9db-e2a9-529f-bd34-5d1b0e8b806a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the product property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155363} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2c2471-556d-59d2-bbbd-0f380cb6a070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155364} {:answer/id #uuid "efddd07e-e955-5b01-80ec-d2b27663684d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155365} {:answer/id #uuid "422676a8-2185-5461-90a7-48b72cc8ef6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef2c2471-556d-59d2-bbbd-0f380cb6a070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155364} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2512859-106c-5a95-83f9-695d7896ce1a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155361} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50284454-a60e-5e53-85e9-08805c980070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1, L2, and L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353369} {:answer/id #uuid "cfda5af2-f4db-58a6-93de-60dcb83160fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1 and L2 only}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50284454-a60e-5e53-85e9-08805c980070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1, L2, and L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353369} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f7ec4e2-4f0e-5de3-b6d8-31abedd07254" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353368} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64cc482-00b7-5fe9-b03f-3a5722bb10d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353372} {:answer/id #uuid "c61b6784-d963-565b-8f89-9b29b295b87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353373} {:answer/id #uuid "0f5b5388-6fba-5672-915a-ba4fc27d6f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c61b6784-d963-565b-8f89-9b29b295b87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353373} :answer-field/id #uuid "77181172-b213-55bf-8754-590132e23f96" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3010e41e-0854-5c29-bb1e-a5d7628e588d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335368" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-x+5,$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,4{x}^{2}=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,-x=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,5=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(3)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-1}}  \nand  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-3}}  \nand  \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\nWe first observe that each term\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2},\\,-x,\\,5\n$$\n\nis bounded near $x=0.$ Thus, each of the lines\n\n$$\n\\text{L1}:\\,4{x}^{2}=O(1),\\,\\text{L2}:\\,-x=O(1),\\,\\text{L3}:\\,5=O(1)\n$$\n\nis valid as $x\\to 0.$\n\nNext, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From lines $\\text{L1},\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$ we know that each term in\n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{2}-x+5\n$$\n\nis $O(1).$ Thus, by lines $\\text{L1, L2, and L3}$ and the $\\text{sum property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(1+1+1)=O(3)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(3)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nSince $3$ is just a constant multiple of $1,$ it follows by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ that\n\n$$\nf=O(1)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe4292f-7653-5486-975e-c206acae734e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of Big-O"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9321d1a-7966-5813-8f4f-53824adae536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353377} {:answer/id #uuid "8cebf043-d2a2-51d3-98e8-cb29d45c6a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353378} {:answer/id #uuid "238faedc-4edf-591b-a924-03267fb55ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353379} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfff8f6-1411-543e-8598-0337e0945799" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353380} {:answer/id #uuid "e62ed3f5-ac97-5525-aa28-18b8e605957b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e62ed3f5-ac97-5525-aa28-18b8e605957b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353381} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2725d9c-6314-57bf-94b8-a7ca6acecca9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353376} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44ef7936-803b-5f7c-a603-d81ced775bc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353383} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7b2f33-d0c1-5b67-8341-7f15d01f81e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44ef7936-803b-5f7c-a603-d81ced775bc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353383} :answer-field/id #uuid "914e97f7-64c7-50e0-906e-856f005240a5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353382} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3025d60f-d12e-5679-a89a-7eda24e9124d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353386} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5c63a1-a4ae-5244-9134-bdf7c4ba3d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353387} {:answer/id #uuid "a99014c9-2bc4-526d-ae11-b9a5e9978942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353388} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0b6a95-cc79-576c-8194-030b45bef940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353389} {:answer/id #uuid "315aff55-cbc8-5ee6-adbf-d987f2971ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3025d60f-d12e-5679-a89a-7eda24e9124d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353386} :answer-field/id #uuid "9935a379-845c-54c0-bc6e-184f2fd6766b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353385} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc282897-9267-53a4-b035-58616315bf47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353392} {:answer/id #uuid "b6822e96-1054-576b-9cea-5f0e5c516771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6822e96-1054-576b-9cea-5f0e5c516771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353393} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ff05e1a-8ce6-50e3-89c0-77d851386487" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0661e27c-b760-5ecd-b8d9-681479e3e254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353395} {:answer/id #uuid "16267336-a53d-5167-9fbb-dc5330d78a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353396} {:answer/id #uuid "f46c1a4a-1b6d-507b-9c2b-17a4fa763e76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353397} {:answer/id #uuid "0f746d5e-357c-5c52-abd4-0de27527ffd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353398} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9e1b63-9047-5481-878e-2f186b9ed273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f746d5e-357c-5c52-abd4-0de27527ffd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353398} :answer-field/id #uuid "9630b68a-3196-5137-8e3c-1d6f38af2c66" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe0fc043-68d7-5d3e-9605-f458a82574a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335468" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)={e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{-x},$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O\\,({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=O\\,({e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,f=O({e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,{e}^{-x}=O(1)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines \n{{field-4}} and \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O\\,({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{-x})\n$$\n\nand this is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From line $\\text{L1},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O\\,({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{-x})\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=O\\,({e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L2}$ and $\\text{the absorption property},$ we have \n\n$$\nf=O\\,({e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{e}^{-x})=O\\,({e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O({e}^{-x})\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x}=O(1).\n$$\n\nThus,  by lines $\\text{L3 and L4}$ and $\\text{the transitive property},$ we have \n\n$$\nf=O(1).\n$$"}]}
